# Do you like to learn on your own or with others?
Personally, I enjoy both ways of studying, depending on the situation. When I study by myself, I can go at my own pace and focus deeply on the subjects that interest me or that I find challenging. I also find it easier to concentrate because I can study without being distracted by other people.

On the other hand, when I study with others, we can exchange ideas and share different perspectives, which can help me understand a subject from a different angle. I also enjoy discussing problems with other people because sometimes they can come up with ideas that I hadn't thought of.

So overall, I prefer studying by myself when I need to concentrate deeply, but I also appreciate studying with others when I want to discuss ideas or solve problems together.

# What's the difference between learning face-to-face with teachers and learning by yourself?
I think there are quite a few differences between learning face-to-face with teachers and studying on your own. When you're in a classroom and have constant interaction with your teacher, it's easier for them to adjust their teaching approach based on students' needs and responses. You can also ask questions immediately when you don't understand something.

But it's a completely different story when you're learning on your own. You basically have to be your own boss and take responsibility for your learning. You have to set your own goals, manage your time effectively, and put in consistent effort to achieve them. So, self-directed learning requires a certain level of self-discipline and patience.

Sometimes, you also have to figure out complex problems on your own, which can be frustrating at times. However, I think it's also quite rewarding because when you finally solve a problem by yourself, you feel a real sense of achievement.
